.\" OpenBSD-style concise man page
.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt MKTEMP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mktemp
.Nd create a temporary file or directory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm mktemp
.Op Fl dqu
.Op Fl p Ar dir
.Op Fl -suffix Ns = Ns Ar suff
.Op Fl t
.Op Ar template
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility creates a temporary file or directory safely and prints its name.
The
.Ar template
must contain at least three consecutive
.Ql X
characters at the end, which are replaced with random alphanumeric
characters to ensure uniqueness.
If no
.Ar template
is specified,
.Pa tmp.XXXXXXXXXX
is used.
.Pp
The file is created with mode 0600 and the directory with mode 0700
to prevent other users from accessing the temporary resource.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d , Fl Fl directory
Create a directory instead of a file.
.It Fl u , Fl Fl dry-run
Do not create anything; merely print a name.
This is inherently unsafe, as another process could create a file
with the same name between the time
.Nm
prints it and the caller uses it.
.It Fl q , Fl Fl quiet
Suppress diagnostic messages about failure to create the file
or directory.
Still exits with a non-zero status on failure.
.It Fl p Ar dir , Fl Fl tmpdir Ns = Ns Ar dir
Use
.Ar dir
as the directory in which to create the temporary resource.
If not specified, the value of the
.Ev TMPDIR
environment variable is used, or
.Pa /tmp
if
.Ev TMPDIR
is not set.
.It Fl -suffix Ns = Ns Ar suff
Append
.Ar suff
to the
.Ar template .
The
.Ar suff
must not contain a directory separator.
.It Fl t
Interpret
.Ar template
relative to the directory specified by
.Fl p
or the
.Ev TMPDIR
environment variable or
.Pa /tmp .
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width TMPDIR
.It Ev TMPDIR
Directory in which to create temporary files.
Defaults to
.Pa /tmp
if not set.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std mktemp
.Sh EXAMPLES
Create a temporary file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mktemp
/tmp/tmp.A1b2C3d4E5
.Ed
.Pp
Create a temporary directory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mktemp -d
/tmp/tmp.X9y8Z7w6V5
.Ed
.Pp
Create a temporary file in a specific directory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mktemp -p /var/tmp myapp.XXXXXX
/var/tmp/myapp.K3m4N5
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mkdir 1 ,
.Xr rm 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is not specified by POSIX but is widely available on
BSD and GNU systems.
This implementation is compatible with GNU coreutils
.Nm .
.Sh AUTHORS
.An "vibeutils implementation by Travis Cole"
